1
00:00:00,440 --> 00:00:02,520
In this video, I'm
going to talk about

2
00:00:02,520 --> 00:00:04,570
read-only root filesystems.

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:06,800
I want to introduce
overlayFS and

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:10,910
tmpfs as ways we can build
read-only root filesystem.

5
00:00:10,910 --> 00:00:13,260
I want to introduce the concept

6
00:00:13,260 --> 00:00:14,640
of read-only root filesystems,

7
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,050
why you want to use it,

8
00:00:16,050 --> 00:00:17,580
and understand some
strategies for

9
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,680
implementing them on
embedded devices.

10
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:25,050
First of all, overlayFS
is a functionality that's

11
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:27,000
supported in recent
Linux kernels

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,205
came in at version 3.8,

13
00:00:29,205 --> 00:00:32,250
that is a really slick
capability that allows us

14
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:35,700
to virtually merge
multiple folders,

15
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,160
keeping the contents of
individual folders separate.

16
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:44,690
What this allows us to do is
take an upper and a lower

17
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:46,910
directory and make
an overlay that

18
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:49,535
includes the content
of both directories.

19
00:00:49,535 --> 00:00:52,040
The upper and lower
directories could

20
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:55,935
be contents that are
on different volumes.

21
00:00:55,935 --> 00:00:57,920
Those volumes could have
different permissions

22
00:00:57,920 --> 00:00:59,810
in terms of whether you
can read and write,

23
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:02,990
and they could be completely
different filesystems

24
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:05,155
on those separate volumes.

25
00:01:05,155 --> 00:01:07,490
The combination of
the upper and lower

26
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:10,280
together gives us the total
content of the overlay.

27
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,290
For instance, you
can see if the lower

28
00:01:12,290 --> 00:01:14,555
had some sparse set
of files like this,

29
00:01:14,555 --> 00:01:17,785
and the upper had some
combination of additional files,

30
00:01:17,785 --> 00:01:19,160
the overlay would get

31
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:22,180
the content from the
upper layer if it exists,

32
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:23,120
otherwise, it would get

33
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:24,490
the content from
the lower layer.

34
00:01:24,490 --> 00:01:27,140
This allows you to
make a modification in

35
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:28,550
the upper layer that
would essentially

36
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:31,050
overwrite the content
in the lower.

37
00:01:31,220 --> 00:01:34,640
Modification to the file in
the overlay that's only in

38
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,585
the lower layer actually
results in two steps.

39
00:01:38,585 --> 00:01:40,730
It results in a copy
of the file up to

40
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:43,340
the upper directory and

41
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,280
then a modification of the
file on the upper directory.

42
00:01:46,280 --> 00:01:48,500
Then if you are
modifying a file that

43
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,720
already exists in
the upper directory,

44
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:54,025
then that file would only
exist in the upper directory,

45
00:01:54,025 --> 00:01:56,870
or that file modification
would just be placed there.

46
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,510
This would allow you to
take the lower directory,

47
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:01,490
make this read-only
for instance,

48
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:06,140
and have this to be the basis
of your filesystem and keep

49
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,975
the contents that are changing

50
00:02:08,975 --> 00:02:13,200
in an upper directory
or an upper filesystem.

51
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:15,615
One thing you might ask is,

52
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:17,160
"What if I wanted to delete

53
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:19,450
a content from the
lower directory?

54
00:02:19,450 --> 00:02:20,720
I wanted to delete content from

55
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:22,610
my filesystem but I

56
00:02:22,610 --> 00:02:24,590
had the lower directory
mounted read-only,

57
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:26,150
for instance, how
would I be able

58
00:02:26,150 --> 00:02:29,015
to remove content from
the lower directory?"

59
00:02:29,015 --> 00:02:30,980
It turns out there's
a clever way to do

60
00:02:30,980 --> 00:02:33,290
that through what they
call a whiteout file,

61
00:02:33,290 --> 00:02:36,200
where it's really a char device

62
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,555
with a node major
and minor of 0,0.

63
00:02:38,555 --> 00:02:41,570
It's a special file type

64
00:02:41,570 --> 00:02:43,580
that's essentially
not useful with

65
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:46,340
this node major and
minor reserved number

66
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,660
that's just a clue to

67
00:02:47,660 --> 00:02:50,510
the overlay filesystem
that what we're

68
00:02:50,510 --> 00:02:51,980
really trying to do is block

69
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:53,915
the content from
the lower layer.

70
00:02:53,915 --> 00:02:56,715
What this would allow us to
do is to delete a content in

71
00:02:56,715 --> 00:02:58,940
the upper layer and make it look

72
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:02,370
like the file was not
in the lower layer.

73
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:05,870
In addition to the
upper and lower layer,

74
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:09,530
there's also a work directory
that has temporary content.

75
00:03:09,530 --> 00:03:11,480
That work directory needs to be

76
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:13,400
an empty directory that's on

77
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:15,880
the same filesystem
as the upperdir.

78
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,740
In order to mount an
overlay filesystem,

79
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:20,450
you can use a command
line like this,

80
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:22,565
where you specify
the lower directory,

81
00:03:22,565 --> 00:03:23,900
the upper directory,

82
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:28,050
and the work directory as the
three different arguments.

83
00:03:29,330 --> 00:03:32,550
Talking about how we'd use

84
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:38,205
overlayFS to implement a
read-only root filesystem,

85
00:03:38,205 --> 00:03:40,490
it's also important
to talk about

86
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:43,010
tmpfs or a temporary
file systems.

87
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:44,840
Temporary file system is really

88
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:46,640
just a place that you can

89
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:48,470
store files that have
a short lifetime,

90
00:03:48,470 --> 00:03:50,210
and specifically
files that don't

91
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:52,285
need to be saved across reboots.

92
00:03:52,285 --> 00:03:54,140
It's really just going
to be a filesystem

93
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:55,745
that's created in RAM.

94
00:03:55,745 --> 00:03:59,570
Tmpfs is a file system type

95
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:01,055
that's supported by
the kernel that's

96
00:04:01,055 --> 00:04:03,175
specifically meant
for this purpose.

97
00:04:03,175 --> 00:04:06,605
If you want to create a
filesystem at mount somepath,

98
00:04:06,605 --> 00:04:08,765
you can do it and have

99
00:04:08,765 --> 00:04:11,120
a RAM-backed copy
of the filesystem.

100
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:14,940
If you just use mount- o
tmpfs tmpfs/mount somepath.

101
00:04:15,590 --> 00:04:17,960
Once you do that, if you access

102
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:19,250
the content and mount somepath,

103
00:04:19,250 --> 00:04:22,775
you'll really just be
writing content to tmpfs,

104
00:04:22,775 --> 00:04:24,110
meaning that the next time you

105
00:04:24,110 --> 00:04:26,620
reboot that content's
going to be gone.

106
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:30,215
The default option creates

107
00:04:30,215 --> 00:04:32,210
a max size of the
filesystem with

108
00:04:32,210 --> 00:04:34,855
half the size of
your physical RAM.

109
00:04:34,855 --> 00:04:36,890
You might want to
restrict that if you know

110
00:04:36,890 --> 00:04:39,440
that you don't want to
use that much space or

111
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,135
wouldn't want an
application to write

112
00:04:41,135 --> 00:04:43,895
gigabytes worth of files
and use up that much RAM.

113
00:04:43,895 --> 00:04:45,380
Then you can mount with

114
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:47,030
a specific size
where, for instance,

115
00:04:47,030 --> 00:04:52,010
here you can specify one
megabyte in the -o options.

116
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:59,380
Talking about read-only root
filesystem, first of all,

117
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:01,734
why would we want to use a
read-only root filesystem

118
00:05:01,734 --> 00:05:03,520
with an embedded device?

119
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:06,250
Read only root filesystem
could be useful

120
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:09,520
to number 1, avoid
unintentional rights.

121
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:11,260
Avoid changing a
file that you didn't

122
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:13,210
intend to change and if

123
00:05:13,210 --> 00:05:14,650
you're using

124
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:18,490
a dual AB redundant update
scheme, for instance,

125
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:19,840
it's probably important to know

126
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:21,280
exactly which file
you're changing

127
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:22,690
because if you're changing

128
00:05:22,690 --> 00:05:24,535
some files in the
root filesystem,

129
00:05:24,535 --> 00:05:25,780
those are probably going to be

130
00:05:25,780 --> 00:05:28,629
lost as a part of
the update process.

131
00:05:28,629 --> 00:05:32,679
You can also reduce filesystem
corruption possibilities.

132
00:05:32,679 --> 00:05:34,645
Anytime you're changing a file,

133
00:05:34,645 --> 00:05:37,015
there's a chance that
if you lose power,

134
00:05:37,015 --> 00:05:38,530
maybe you could end up with

135
00:05:38,530 --> 00:05:40,555
corrupt contents of
the file system.

136
00:05:40,555 --> 00:05:43,450
Most modern file systems have
ways to recover from this,

137
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:45,085
but those aren't foolproof.

138
00:05:45,085 --> 00:05:47,080
There's still chances
they could fail.

139
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:48,970
Anytime you're
allowing modifications

140
00:05:48,970 --> 00:05:50,605
of your file system,

141
00:05:50,605 --> 00:05:52,780
you're opening yourself
up to the possibility

142
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:55,465
that you may end up with
filesystem corruption.

143
00:05:55,465 --> 00:05:57,040
Making our root filesystem

144
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:58,600
read-only just
removes the chance

145
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:02,185
that we'd be modifying that
during normal operations.

146
00:06:02,185 --> 00:06:03,940
It also can simplify the

147
00:06:03,940 --> 00:06:06,160
factory default
reset strategies.

148
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:09,970
Again, because we're moving
the content of whatever we

149
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:11,920
change at runtime outside of

150
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:14,155
the content that boots
and runs our device,

151
00:06:14,155 --> 00:06:17,140
it becomes easier to just
focus on removing content from

152
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,480
that data partition in the case

153
00:06:19,480 --> 00:06:20,260
that we need to go back to

154
00:06:20,260 --> 00:06:22,015
the factory default
configuration.

155
00:06:22,015 --> 00:06:24,970
You don't need to figure
out which values changed in

156
00:06:24,970 --> 00:06:26,860
the root filesystem selectively

157
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:29,695
delete or rollback
individual files.

158
00:06:29,695 --> 00:06:33,070
It can also simplify
device updates strategies.

159
00:06:33,070 --> 00:06:35,065
For the reason I
mentioned before,

160
00:06:35,065 --> 00:06:36,670
if you're going
to be using an AB

161
00:06:36,670 --> 00:06:38,110
update scheme, for instance,

162
00:06:38,110 --> 00:06:40,240
you want to know that you
can completely overwrite

163
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:42,670
one partition and boot from

164
00:06:42,670 --> 00:06:45,070
that partition and you'll
get the behavior you

165
00:06:45,070 --> 00:06:47,635
expect and one way to
make sure that is to

166
00:06:47,635 --> 00:06:51,310
make sure that the
root partition

167
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:54,230
is never modified during
normal operation.

168
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:58,435
Talking specifically about using

169
00:06:58,435 --> 00:07:00,775
read-only root
filesystem for update,

170
00:07:00,775 --> 00:07:02,860
if we're going back and forth

171
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:05,860
between a partition
A and partition B,

172
00:07:05,860 --> 00:07:07,450
we're going to be
completely replacing

173
00:07:07,450 --> 00:07:09,910
the content of the root
filesystem partition.

174
00:07:09,910 --> 00:07:12,190
It really never makes
sense to allow writes

175
00:07:12,190 --> 00:07:14,305
to the root filesystem
at runtime,

176
00:07:14,305 --> 00:07:15,940
because anything that you

177
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:17,650
wrote to one of
these partitions,

178
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:20,170
if you tried to write and
boot to the new partition,

179
00:07:20,170 --> 00:07:21,940
it would be lost and

180
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:24,335
a part of the inactive
boot partition.

181
00:07:24,335 --> 00:07:26,190
You want to make sure
that all your changes

182
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:28,710
go into a data partition anyway.

183
00:07:28,710 --> 00:07:33,310
Read-only root filesystem is
a good way to enforce that.

184
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:36,630
The amount options to

185
00:07:36,630 --> 00:07:38,310
the kernel and kernel
boot parameters

186
00:07:38,310 --> 00:07:39,810
that support mounting

187
00:07:39,810 --> 00:07:42,510
the root device read-only
on boot and you

188
00:07:42,510 --> 00:07:45,865
can modify the boot
configuration.

189
00:07:45,865 --> 00:07:46,900
You have to look
at your hardware

190
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:48,745
platform to see how to do this.

191
00:07:48,745 --> 00:07:51,400
On the Raspberry Pi,
there's the commandline.txt

192
00:07:51,400 --> 00:07:54,205
file where you can modify
boot configuration.

193
00:07:54,205 --> 00:07:55,780
You can just specify it,

194
00:07:55,780 --> 00:07:57,250
to include this
parameter as part

195
00:07:57,250 --> 00:07:59,290
of your kernel
boot instructions.

196
00:07:59,290 --> 00:08:03,520
But that's probably not
enough because you need

197
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:09,250
to do some additional work

198
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:11,140
for applications
that might expect to

199
00:08:11,140 --> 00:08:13,510
be able to write to
the root filesystem.

200
00:08:13,510 --> 00:08:15,865
For instance, if
you're using dropbear,

201
00:08:15,865 --> 00:08:17,050
one of the first
things is going to

202
00:08:17,050 --> 00:08:18,130
happen when you boot up is that

203
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:20,725
dropbear is going
to create SSH keys.

204
00:08:20,725 --> 00:08:24,085
If your root filesystem
is read-only,

205
00:08:24,085 --> 00:08:26,740
then that path that
it uses to write

206
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:30,175
those SSH keys will
be writable on boot,

207
00:08:30,175 --> 00:08:34,120
and your SSH wouldn't
work, for instance.

208
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:36,400
Same thing with DHCP.

209
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:38,665
Part of the process
of obtaining a,

210
00:08:38,665 --> 00:08:42,790
an IP address through
DHCP would be to obtain

211
00:08:42,790 --> 00:08:44,350
the devices address
and then modify

212
00:08:44,350 --> 00:08:47,245
some content in the root
filesystem to have that address.

213
00:08:47,245 --> 00:08:49,555
That's not going to
work properly if

214
00:08:49,555 --> 00:08:52,660
the root filesystem
is read-only.

215
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:55,855
There'll be some work to do
to configure these packages.

216
00:08:55,855 --> 00:08:57,430
Then in general, any software

217
00:08:57,430 --> 00:09:00,955
configuration that the user
could modify on your device,

218
00:09:00,955 --> 00:09:03,055
given the example before about

219
00:09:03,055 --> 00:09:05,590
configuring a Wi-Fi access
point, for instance,

220
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:07,975
anything your application
needs to customize,

221
00:09:07,975 --> 00:09:10,180
you need to make sure that
that's not going to be written

222
00:09:10,180 --> 00:09:14,090
to a file that maps to your
read-only root filesystem.

223
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,950
How can we handle
a read-only root

224
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:19,600
filesystem and still be able

225
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,870
to modify some of the content
of the root filesystem?

226
00:09:22,870 --> 00:09:24,910
I'm going to go through
a few different options

227
00:09:24,910 --> 00:09:26,695
and strategies you
can use for that.

228
00:09:26,695 --> 00:09:28,750
But ultimately, we're
going to need to split

229
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:31,255
some storage needs
into categories.

230
00:09:31,255 --> 00:09:35,140
We'll use the read-only root
filesystem for the content

231
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:38,785
that's not going to change at
anytime other than update,

232
00:09:38,785 --> 00:09:40,420
and then we'll have places that

233
00:09:40,420 --> 00:09:41,995
we could store persistent data,

234
00:09:41,995 --> 00:09:44,140
as well as data that's
volatile in the sense

235
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:47,015
that it can be created or
updated at every boot.

236
00:09:47,015 --> 00:09:49,155
One option would be
you could just take

237
00:09:49,155 --> 00:09:52,380
specific locations of your
read-only root filesystem and

238
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:54,690
use symbolic links
to link those into

239
00:09:54,690 --> 00:09:57,425
the appropriate
storage locations.

240
00:09:57,425 --> 00:09:58,870
You could have a data partition

241
00:09:58,870 --> 00:10:00,520
with read/write persistent data,

242
00:10:00,520 --> 00:10:01,690
and you could symbol link for

243
00:10:01,690 --> 00:10:03,325
new read-only root filesystem

244
00:10:03,325 --> 00:10:05,710
the collect directories
or files into

245
00:10:05,710 --> 00:10:08,635
that read/write
persistent data storage.

246
00:10:08,635 --> 00:10:10,630
Same with volatile content that

247
00:10:10,630 --> 00:10:12,730
you're going to
recreate on every boot.

248
00:10:12,730 --> 00:10:15,670
Maybe DHCP
configuration would be

249
00:10:15,670 --> 00:10:16,960
an example of something that

250
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:19,060
you would recreate
on every boot.

251
00:10:19,060 --> 00:10:22,045
That you could symbol
link into tmpfs then

252
00:10:22,045 --> 00:10:24,970
and make that a part of your
read-only root filesystem,

253
00:10:24,970 --> 00:10:28,750
or just link into a tmpfs
partition that you set up.

254
00:10:28,750 --> 00:10:35,290
A second option would be
that you could use OverlayFS

255
00:10:35,290 --> 00:10:38,230
for this implementation and

256
00:10:38,230 --> 00:10:39,880
place your lower layer on

257
00:10:39,880 --> 00:10:41,740
the read-only root
filesystem partition,

258
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:44,860
and then you could place
OverlayFS mounts on any of

259
00:10:44,860 --> 00:10:48,670
the root filesystem
directories that need overlay,

260
00:10:48,670 --> 00:10:52,390
and you could place that upper
level either on tmpfs for

261
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:54,100
volatile content or in

262
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:57,260
a data partition here
for persistent data.

263
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:01,060
There are some things that
could help us implement

264
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:03,760
this with Yocto, in particular.

265
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:07,075
Yocto supports an
EXTRA_IMAGE_FEATURES read-only-rootfs

266
00:11:07,075 --> 00:11:09,175
that automates a lot
of this setup for you.

267
00:11:09,175 --> 00:11:11,710
It has a VOLATILE _BINDS

268
00:11:11,710 --> 00:11:15,130
definition that lets
you specify where

269
00:11:15,130 --> 00:11:17,950
locations on your root
filesystem should mount

270
00:11:17,950 --> 00:11:20,620
to in terms of content

271
00:11:20,620 --> 00:11:23,050
in a volatile or
a data partition

272
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:26,960
for either tmpfs or
persistent data.

273
00:11:28,080 --> 00:11:31,765
A second thing that's related to

274
00:11:31,765 --> 00:11:33,910
running from a root
filesystem or can

275
00:11:33,910 --> 00:11:35,950
be depending on how
you implement it,

276
00:11:35,950 --> 00:11:37,855
is the chroot command.

277
00:11:37,855 --> 00:11:40,120
Chroot is a command
that can be used

278
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:42,475
to change your root directory.

279
00:11:42,475 --> 00:11:46,150
Software that expects to
access absolute paths and

280
00:11:46,150 --> 00:11:49,855
uses the starting
slash or any subpath,

281
00:11:49,855 --> 00:11:52,710
if you use the chroot command,

282
00:11:52,710 --> 00:11:54,570
what you can do is
redirect that software to

283
00:11:54,570 --> 00:12:00,135
a completely separate location
on your storage device.

284
00:12:00,135 --> 00:12:03,160
This is really useful
if you want to

285
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:06,130
switch your root filesystem
from one system to another,

286
00:12:06,130 --> 00:12:08,125
or from one location to another.

287
00:12:08,125 --> 00:12:09,910
For instance, if
you want to have

288
00:12:09,910 --> 00:12:13,195
a root filesystem that's
located on tmpfs,

289
00:12:13,195 --> 00:12:15,880
and then let's say
you want to switch to

290
00:12:15,880 --> 00:12:17,590
a different location in

291
00:12:17,590 --> 00:12:20,365
tmpfs or a different location
on your flash mount,

292
00:12:20,365 --> 00:12:23,170
you could use chroot
for this to specify

293
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:24,340
that your new root location

294
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:26,830
is at some different
mount point.

295
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:29,755
A lot of times whenever
you do a chroot,

296
00:12:29,755 --> 00:12:31,330
you'll require bind mounts or

297
00:12:31,330 --> 00:12:33,415
remounts of temporary
filesystems.

298
00:12:33,415 --> 00:12:36,100
Remember, we've talked
before about the fact that

299
00:12:36,100 --> 00:12:37,975
the Filesystem
Hierarchy Standard

300
00:12:37,975 --> 00:12:39,850
is something really
for user space.

301
00:12:39,850 --> 00:12:42,655
The kernel doesn't care too
much where you're mounting

302
00:12:42,655 --> 00:12:46,510
these directories like
proc insist in dev,

303
00:12:46,510 --> 00:12:48,340
and so we can really

304
00:12:48,340 --> 00:12:50,290
mount those locations
wherever we want,

305
00:12:50,290 --> 00:12:52,150
for instance, under
the directory

306
00:12:52,150 --> 00:12:54,740
that we're going to
create as our new chroot.

307
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:59,095
We can use the chroot

308
00:12:59,095 --> 00:13:01,840
to implement a third option

309
00:13:01,840 --> 00:13:03,460
for read-only root filesystems,

310
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:05,620
where we overlay the
entire root filesystem

311
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:07,450
with another partition.

312
00:13:07,450 --> 00:13:10,360
Rather than having to
think about which file

313
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:12,850
we will be modifying out
of the root filesystem,

314
00:13:12,850 --> 00:13:16,225
we can just completely overlay
the entire root filesystem

315
00:13:16,225 --> 00:13:18,415
with an overlay implementation

316
00:13:18,415 --> 00:13:20,140
that's on a writable partition.

317
00:13:20,140 --> 00:13:22,720
The benefit of this one is
that you don't need to know in

318
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:25,405
advance which of the directories
need to be writable,

319
00:13:25,405 --> 00:13:27,145
you can just let the OverlayFS

320
00:13:27,145 --> 00:13:29,230
handle enabling rights for

321
00:13:29,230 --> 00:13:31,240
whatever files need
to be written.

322
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:33,460
The drawback of this
one is that it's a

323
00:13:33,460 --> 00:13:35,200
little bit more
difficult to set up,

324
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:37,900
because you need a
chroot system that's

325
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:40,840
running on startup from
your initramfs that

326
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:46,930
can do a dance to go from
RAM to overlay root FS,

327
00:13:46,930 --> 00:13:48,940
and set up your overlay root FS

328
00:13:48,940 --> 00:13:51,685
from your initial RAM disk.

329
00:13:51,685 --> 00:13:54,310
It's a little bit more
difficult to set up,

330
00:13:54,310 --> 00:13:56,755
but there are some scripts
that are available to do that.

331
00:13:56,755 --> 00:13:59,080
There's a script that
I linked here that

332
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:01,630
is a raspy and implementation
that you could

333
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:03,175
use for Raspberry Pi

334
00:14:03,175 --> 00:14:07,820
to help you implement this on
Raspberry Pi, if you like.